WEBVTT

00:00:00.705 --> 00:00:03.323
A vision network has millions of weights.

00:00:03.343 --> 00:00:05.822
To learn, every weight needs one number:

00:00:05.842 --> 00:00:08.855
if it moves a little, how much does the loss
change?

00:00:08.945 --> 00:00:14.088
You could nudge each weight and rerun the
whole network: millions of forward passes.

00:00:14.108 --> 00:00:17.770
Backpropagation gets every answer from one
backward sweep.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.818
Shrink it to one path.

00:00:19.838 --> 00:00:21.268
Input x is two.

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:22.930
Times w one, a half.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.242
Plus b one, zero.

00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:25.242
A ReLu.

00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:27.492
Times w two, which is one.

00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:30.875
Then a loss: half the squared miss from the
target, three.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.082
Run it forward.

00:00:32.102 --> 00:00:33.845
Two times a half is one.

00:00:33.865 --> 00:00:37.995
The ReLu passes positives, so h is one, and
the guess is one.

00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:42.140
It misses three by two, so the loss, half of
two squared, is two.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.622
Now go backward.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:47.585
Each node knows only its own local derivative,
the orange chip.

00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:51.405
At the loss, that's the guess minus the
target: minus two.

00:00:51.485 --> 00:00:54.165
Carry it left, multiplying by each chip.

00:00:54.185 --> 00:00:56.865
Into w two, times h: minus two.

00:00:56.885 --> 00:00:59.540
Into h, times w two: minus two.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.515
The ReLu is open, so times one: minus two.

00:01:02.535 --> 00:01:05.210
Into b one, times one: minus two.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.730
One step is left.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.592
The chip on w one is x, which is two.

00:01:09.612 --> 00:01:11.768
So what is the gradient for w one?

00:01:11.788 --> 00:01:13.450
Pause and decide.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.870
Minus two times two: minus four.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.170
Check it the slow way: raise w one by a
hundredth,

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.870
and the loss falls by about four hundredths.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:29.840
Backprop got every gradient in one sweep, for
about the cost of a forward pass.

00:01:30.295 --> 00:01:31.925
Real networks branch.

00:01:31.945 --> 00:01:35.875
Say h also feeds a box predictor, which sends
back plus one.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:40.570
Where wires meet, gradients add: minus two
plus one is minus one.

00:01:40.955 --> 00:01:42.260
Now a surprise.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.173
Set b one to minus two.

00:01:44.193 --> 00:01:47.223
Then z is minus one, and the ReLu shuts.

00:01:47.242 --> 00:01:49.235
The loss grows to four point five,

00:01:49.255 --> 00:01:53.773
yet every weight gets a zero gradient: the
shut gate blocks both directions.

00:01:53.792 --> 00:01:56.080
A dead ReLu learns nothing.

00:01:56.615 --> 00:02:01.595
Reset b one to zero, and take one gradient
step of size zero point one.

00:02:01.615 --> 00:02:06.158
Run forward again: the loss falls from two to
zero point one eight.

00:02:06.178 --> 00:02:11.265
A CNN is this same graph with bigger nodes,
and autograd builds it for you.

00:02:11.695 --> 00:02:12.988
Remember three things.

00:02:13.008 --> 00:02:15.138
Every node has a local derivative.

00:02:15.158 --> 00:02:17.175
Along a path, multiply them.

00:02:17.195 --> 00:02:18.945
Where paths meet, add.

